Z||000000||Dslice||12-25-2001||03:47 PM||||I need to disable, preferably temporarily the rear breaks on my 84 coup because one of the rear calipers is leaking and needs to be replaced. But I need to get it drivable to get to work. Any one know the best way to do this with minimal materials. It's Xmas and nothings open plus I cant drive anywhere anyhow.<P>Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Rick<P>86GT<BR>84COUP<BR>http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt||||reg||4
Z||000001||honegod||12-25-2001||06:49 PM||||it is nasty, but it will work for a short time.<P>remove the hose from the caliper, use a nut and bolt the same size as the hollow bolt that the brake fluid goes through.<P>if you have a bolt that is EXACTLY the same as the hollow bolt, simply swap it in, no fluid will get to the caliper.<P>a nut and bolt that is roughly the same size can be used, but you have to secure the hose end that you just bolted shut, because it will be flopping around loose, I'd ziptie it to the strut.<P>if you use the nut and bolt, be sure to use the copper washers to help seal, and prevent damage to the hose end. it might well leak a little, but so what, it'll get you there 'till you can fix it right.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierodoug5||12-25-2001||08:23 PM||||get something to clamp off the brake line, Visegrip, brake line clamp etc. It will damage the line if you leave it on long, but will keep you driving. Don't tailgate with only 3 calipers, and hold the wheel with 2 hands!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dslice||12-25-2001||11:14 PM||||Thanks, <BR>JaeiSun found me a replacement caliper so hopefully that will fix my problem.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Rick<P>86GT<BR>84COUP<BR>http://home.mn.rr.com/gmfierogt||||reg||1
